From: Freifunk im Neanderland <projekt@neanderfunk.de>
Subject: client: reinit back-off that actually waits, no ioctl on a missing tunnel interface

A node with the mesh VPN enabled but no WAN uplink (mesh only via LAN or
WLAN) keeps the CPU busy: load 1.5 on a TL-WDR3600, 0 % idle, three
quarters of it in the kernel, about 1000 process starts per minute. On a
64 MB Archer C25 the same loop reads modprobe from flash every time.

Two things add up:

1. context_reinitialize() always ends with context_delete_tunnel(), which
   does ioctl(SIOCGIFFLAGS) on the tunnel interface (mesh-vpn). Without a
   broker that interface never exists, and for a missing interface the
   kernel's dev_load() calls request_module("netdev-mesh-vpn") and
   request_module("mesh-vpn"). The client waits for both modprobe runs
   (seen as D state in call_usermodehelper_exec). Measured on the WDR3600:
   115 modprobe runs in 30 s, all of them waited for by tunneldigger.

2. The 2 s back-off in STATE_REINIT never applies: context_reinitialize()
   sets timer_reinit to 0, so is_timeout(&timer_reinit, 2) is true at
   once. Without WAN the local resolver fails immediately and every broker
   context cycles reinit -> resolve -> reinit; only the modprobe runs slow
   it down.

Check /sys/class/net/<iface> before the ioctl, and start the back-off at
reinit time, so a context waits the intended 2 s before trying again.

Signed-off-by: Freifunk im Neanderland <projekt@neanderfunk.de>
---
--- a/client/l2tp_client.c
+++ b/client/l2tp_client.c
@@ -476,7 +476,10 @@
   ctx->timer_usage = 0;
   ctx->timer_cookie = 0;
   ctx->timer_tunnel = 0;
-  ctx->timer_reinit = 0;
+  // Start the reinit back-off now. With 0, is_timeout() in STATE_REINIT fired
+  // at once, and without WAN (DNS fails immediately) the context looped
+  // through reinit/resolve as fast as the CPU allowed.
+  ctx->timer_reinit = now;
   ctx->timer_keepalive = now;
   ctx->timer_resolving = -1;
 
@@ -911,11 +914,15 @@
 
 void context_delete_tunnel(l2tp_context *ctx)
 {
-  // Take the interface down.
+  // Take the interface down, if there is one. Check sysfs first: an ioctl on
+  // a missing interface makes the kernel call request_module("netdev-<if>")
+  // and request_module("<if>") (dev_load), i.e. two modprobe runs per reinit.
+  char sysfs_path[IFNAMSIZ + 16];
+  snprintf(sysfs_path, sizeof(sysfs_path), "/sys/class/net/%s", ctx->tunnel_iface);
   struct ifreq ifr;
   memset(&ifr, 0, sizeof(ifr));
   strncpy(ifr.ifr_name, ctx->tunnel_iface, sizeof(ifr.ifr_name));
-  if (ioctl(ctx->fd, SIOCGIFFLAGS, &ifr) == 0) {
+  if (access(sysfs_path, F_OK) == 0 && ioctl(ctx->fd, SIOCGIFFLAGS, &ifr) == 0) {
     ifr.ifr_flags &= ~IFF_UP;
     if (ioctl(ctx->fd, SIOCSIFFLAGS, &ifr) < 0) {
       syslog(LOG_WARNING, "[%s:%s] Failed to take down interface %s (errno=%d).",
